digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56622-2
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes and validates UNITO, a convolutional image-segmentation framework that transforms bivariate cytometry data into density images, learns hierarchical pre-gating and gating boundaries from expert annotations, and automatically performs adaptive pixel-level contouring to identify cell populations across cytometric modalities.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A method that converts bivariate cytometry measurements into density images can use convolutional image-segmentation outputs as adaptive pixel-level contours for expert-like gating."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Cytometric analysis has gained tremendous attention in immunological experiments because it provides reliable, high-throughput single-cell measurements."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Because no software configures hierarchical bivariate pre-gating and gating tasks, the tree-like structure of those tasks cannot be automated by current software."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Across cytometry history, gating practice, manual labor, unsupervised-method limits, pre-gating assumptions, lack of adaptive pre-gating, and lack of hierarchical automation, there is an unmet need for automated methods that handle bivariate hierarchical pre-gating and gating."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Convolutional kernels can help handle variance in protein-expression data for gating through translational invariance and equivariance."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Image segmentation on bivariate density maps can mimic the global density view that human experts use for automated gating."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Deep learning with convolutional image segmentation is a suitable technical direction for cytometric density-map gating because it combines cytometry-domain deep learning, variance handling, global density-view modeling, and pixel-level prediction."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) The unmet need for automated hierarchical cytometry gating is plausibly addressed by applying convolutional image segmentation to cytometric density-map representations."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Flow cytometry has advanced since its 1960s introduction by increasing measurement speed and the number of measurable parameters for immune-cell analysis."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) Because manual gating is time-consuming and labor-intensive, algorithms have been developed to attempt gating automation."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Automatic gating methods must accommodate sample-preparation and instrument-driven fluctuations in protein detection and population shape."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) Convolutional image methods provide a basis for global-view and pixel-level prediction on density-map representations."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Mass cytometry enables precise multi-parameter analysis with minimal spillover by using heavy-metal tags and avoiding fluorescence compensation."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Flow cytometry and mass cytometry use different labeling strategies that produce different signal-handling and spillover characteristics."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) When gating requires manually defining boundaries for each cell subtype and each sample, that manual boundary-definition process is time-consuming and labor-intensive."];
  "c10" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is assumption of pre-gating (s15,s17), and if..., then major challenge for full automation."];
  "c11" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is no adaptive pre-gating (s16,s18,s19), and if..., then manual labor persists."];
  "c13" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is DL effective (s22), and if..., then DL can address automation (but existing methods assume pre-gated data s24)."];
  "c5" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is gating as boundary on bivariate density and hierarchical (s7,s8), and if..., then both flow and mass cytometry employ similar gating procedures despite different technologies."];
  "c9" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is lack of reproduction/adaptation (s13), and if..., then hard to explain results."];
  "common_contrast" [label="(0,0,0) Common pattern: Different labeling strategies in cytometry lead to distinct signal handling and aggregate detection approaches."];
  "common_knowledge" [label="(0,0,0) Common pattern: Deep learning combined with convolutional image segmentation provides global identification, pixel-level boundaries, translational invariance to variance, and hierarchical interpretability."];
  "common_problem" [label="(0,0,0) Common pattern: Cytometric methods (despite evolution s1-s9) suffer from manual labor, unsupervised limitations, pre-gating assumptions, variance, and lack of hierarchical automation."];
  "r1" [label="(0,0,0) If a method produces reliable, high-throughput single-cell measurements in immunological experiments, then it gains tremendous attention."];
  "r2" [label="(0,0,0) If flow cytometry evolved with increased speed and parameters since the 1960s, then cytometry technology has advanced significantly for complex analysis."];
  "r3" [label="(0,0,0) If mass cytometry was developed in 2009 to include more parameters and avoid fluorescence compensation, then it enables precise multi-parameter analysis with minimal spillover."];
  "r_algo" [label="(0,0,0) If manual gating is time-consuming and labor-intensive, then algorithms are developed to attempt automation."];
  "r_cnn" [label="(0,0,0) If convolutional kernels provide translational invariance and equivariance, then they can handle variance in protein expression data for gating."];
  "r_dl" [label="(0,0,0) If deep learning disentangles complex relationships in cytometric domains (s22,s23), then it is promising for gating tasks."];
  "r_gating" [label="(0,0,0) If gating is defined on bivariate density maps and configured hierarchically to mimic immune cell differentiation (s7,s8), then it is the standard procedure for cell type identification in both technologies (s9)."];
  "r_global" [label="(0,0,0) If human experts use global density view, then image segmentation on bivariate density maps can mimic this for automation."];
  "r_hier" [label="(0,0,0) If no software configures hierarchical bivariate pre-gating/gating, then the tree-like structure cannot be automated."];
  "r_image" [label="(0,0,0) If convolutional architecture detects regardless of position and adapts to segmentation (s34-s36), then it provides global view and pixel-level prediction on density maps."];
  "r_manual" [label="(0,0,0) If manually defining boundaries for each cell subtype and each sample, then the process is time-consuming and labor-intensive."];
  "r_pregate1" [label="(0,0,0) If methods assume pre-gating has been performed, then they do not address pre-gating automatically."];
  "r_pregate2" [label="(0,0,0) If no efficient open-source adaptive pre-gating methods exist, then current approaches require manual pre-gating and human input for parameters."];
  "r_unsup2" [label="(0,0,0) If unsupervised methods lack reproduction of expert gating and adaptation to domain knowledge, then they cannot satisfactorily explain clustering results."];
  "r_variance" [label="(0,0,0) If technical/biological variance causes fluctuations in detection and shape (s25,s26), then methods must accommodate variance for accurate gating."];
  "s1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently there is: Cytometric analysis produces reliable, high-throughput measurements of single cells and has gained tremendous attention in immunological experiments."];
  "s10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently there is: Manually defining boundaries for each cell subtype and each sample."];
  "s13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently there is: Unsupervised methods lack the ability to reproduce expert gating and adapt to domain knowledge."];
  "s15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently there is: Methods assume single cells have been separated from doublets/debris (pre-gating)."];
  "s16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently there is: Efficient open-source adaptive pre-gating methods do not exist."];
  "s2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently there is: Flow cytometry was introduced in the 1960s to separate/count immune cell subtypes and underwent critical evolution with increased speed and parameters."];
  "s20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently there is: No software can configure a series of hierarchical bivariate pre-gating and gating tasks."];
  "s22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently there is: Deep learning has been effective in disentangling complex relationships in cytometric domains including cell type identification."];
  "s26" [label="(26,0,0) Currently there is: Sample preparation and instrument variation cause fluctuations in protein detection and population shape (even with constant protocol)."];
  "s3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently there is: Mass cytometry (cytometry by time-of-flight) was developed in 2009 to include more parameters and avoid fluorescence compensation."];
  "s33" [label="(33,0,0) Currently there is: Existing methods are suboptimal for biological variance, but deep neural networks with convolutional kernels address challenges via translational invariance and equivariance."];
  "s34" [label="(34,0,0) Currently there is: Convolutional architecture was originally designed for image classification tasks."];
  "s37" [label="(37,0,0) Currently there is: Manual gating is challenging for automated software because human experts have a global view of cell density to inspect/quantify types."];
  "s4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently there is: Flow cytometry labels antibodies with fluorophores whose emission spectra overlap, causing spillover."];
  "s7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently there is: Gating is the most important and widely used method of subtyping cell populations as a boundary/polygon on a bivariate density map of two protein measurements."];
